8065c61fdeef0bab337aef57014bffbfe1a8ecf472f6da1f6a14204ff456b8a6   78576  lib/libw80.a
a00bb9ecd9ad9e7c58d9f9c4efa407b92ba129d0535c80aef7f13a840b09b71e   10708  lib/web51_23.obj
ff0c60de66ebcd6a08ab7346039f1499fa5dcc29edb365213298065ca0e4b0a1   10280  lib/web51_80.obj
dae56bcbf5f68928779f6e9c2bdc8edd055694558603ab42d9e746d1c08cf2ea   55332  cgi/libcgi.a
plus the 26 cgi/*.obj, whose base.7z hashes are the "STAGED OUTPUTS" block of
tb/objects-report/PROVENANCE.

Every one of these now has a provenance chain back to the 2001 originals in
this archive. The 26 cgi/*.obj and lib/libk80.a in base.7z are gnu13's own
objects run through tb/i51elf_le2be.py + tb/i51elf_sym_uc.py, byte-identical
after conversion. lib/web51_80.obj, lib/web51_23.obj and lib/libw80.a were
re-assembled copies until they were replaced by the same conversion of the
overlay's originals; the archives go through tb/i51elf_ar.py, which runs the
two steps per member and reproduces lib/libk80.a, lib/libk23.a and
lib/libw23.a from this archive byte-for-byte.

lib/libk23.a and lib/libw23.a in the overlay are byte-identical to
lib/lib23_old/libk23.a and lib/lib23_old/libw23.a already shipped inside
base.7z, which is a second, independent copy of the same 2001 files.


------------------------------------------------------------------------
4. KNOWN CONTENT DELTAS - overlay vs base.7z, beyond format
------------------------------------------------------------------------
These paths are not just a re-encoding of one another; the bytes differ beyond
endianness and symbol case.

lib/web51_80.obj, lib/web51_23.obj and lib/libw80.a used to be listed here.
base.7z shipped re-assembled copies of them, and a re-assembly loses what the
port's assembler could not write: an external memory-space common came out as
SHN_COMMON instead of the SHN_I51_* index naming its space. Counted over the
symbol tables, the three lost 0xff02 x1 + 0xff06 x6, 0xff02 x1 + 0xff06 x8 and
0xff00 x11 + 0xff02 x2 + 0xff03 x2 + 0xff06 x21 respectively, every one of them
turning into a plain common the linker then placed in byte-addressed .bss.
They now hold the overlay's own objects converted through
tb/i51elf_le2be.py + tb/i51elf_sym_uc.py (tb/i51elf_ar.py for the archive), so
the only remaining difference is the format the conversion exists to change.

  cgi/libcgi.a  55792 (overlay) vs 55332 (base.7z)
  lib/libk23.a, lib/libw23.a - member-level comparison not carried out; the
    overlay copies are the same bytes base.7z already carries in lib/lib23_old.

Paths base.7z ships that the overlay does NOT replace, because gnu13 has no
counterpart and no project links them: lib/web51.obj (a duplicate of
base.7z's lib/web51_80.obj), lib/libw.a, and the stray
"cgi/$(basename  .asm).obj" left behind by a Makefile expansion. They stay in
the current object format inside a frozen run and are never opened.


------------------------------------------------------------------------
5. WHAT THE OVERLAY DELIBERATELY LEAVES ALONE
------------------------------------------------------------------------
gnu13 also carries the .asm sources, the perl generators, include/param.inc,
lib/www51.sc and the project directories, and those differ from base.7z's:

  - .section flags were rewritten from `, #alloc` to `, "ax"` throughout the
    sources and inside bin/html2db.pl. Both spellings parse in 2.11.2 gas
    (obj_elf_section_word and obj_elf_parse_section_letters respectively);
    "ax" additionally sets SHF_EXECINSTR, which only affects orphan placement.
  - include/param.inc uses .equ where 2001 used .set, and guards ACTIVESTACK
    and DUALSTACK with .ifndef.
  - lib/www51.sc comments out *(reset_network), uppercases the interrupt
    trampoline symbol to _RETI_, and adds INTIE0.. and STACK aliases next to
    the lowercase intIE0.. and stack ones.

The overlay keeps base.7z's versions of the sources and the generators on
purpose. The reference ROMs in base.7z were linked from them, so swapping in
gnu13's would change what a frozen run is being compared against rather than
tell us anything about the toolchain. lib/www51.sc is the one exception, and
only for the symbol name - section 6.


------------------------------------------------------------------------
6. lib/www51.sc - THE ONE DERIVED FILE
------------------------------------------------------------------------
The overlay's copy is base.7z's own lib/www51.sc with the ten occurrences of
_RETI_ rewritten to _reti_, and nothing else:

  python -c "import sys;d=open(sys.argv[1],'rb').read();
             open(sys.argv[2],'wb').write(d.replace(b'_RETI_',b'_reti_'))" \
      base.7z:lib/www51.sc  overlay:lib/www51.sc

  base.7z    b4b89651391bedd81b31373588ac70a897fcea5e7fed2bc6c38ace907e548099
  overlay    aeb3d964ab51a63ebec33fbc9d4d570c75ef7ccd65f1efec08b95ec5374c85b4
  both 5629 bytes, 265 LF-terminated lines

Why. The script's ten PROVIDE lines for the unused interrupt vectors all
resolve to the trampoline in web51_80.obj / web51_23.obj. That symbol is
spelled _reti_ in the 2001 objects and _RETI_ in base.7z's, because the
current assembler uppercases every symbol it emits - which is what
tb/i51elf_sym_uc.py replays when converting an old object to the new format.
Left alone, a frozen link stops with

  lib/www51.sc: undefined symbol `_RETI_' referenced in expression

on all ten projects. Rewriting the reference is the script-side half of the
same case conversion the objects went through; the uppercase alias names
INTIE0.., which nothing in a frozen run references, are left in place.

What was NOT done, deliberately: gnu13's own lib/www51.sc was not used. It
still has *(reset_network) live, where base.7z's has it commented out, so it
would place a section that the reference ROMs were linked without. That is a
testbench decision, not an encoding difference, and it applies to the frozen
and the current line alike. Keeping base.7z's script leaves exactly one
variable between the two lines: the toolchain and the object encoding it
reads.
